.TH NL 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
nl \- number the lines of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nl
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR style ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR incr ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR sep ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR start ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nl
writes each
.I file
to standard output, adding a line number to the start of each line that
it numbers.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still written.
.PP
A numbered line is its number in decimal, right-aligned in a field of at
least
.I width
columns, then the separator, then the line.
A line that is not numbered gets spaces in place of the number and
separator, as many as the field width plus the length of the separator
in bytes.
A line that is not numbered does not advance the count.
.PP
By default only nonempty lines are numbered.
The first number is 1, and each numbered line adds 1 to the next number.
The count continues from one file to the next.
.B \-b
chooses the lines to number, and
.B \-v
and
.B \-i
change the first number and the step.
.SS Lines
A line ends at an LF.
A CR just before the LF is removed, so CR LF line endings are accepted.
A NUL byte also ends a line and is removed, and an LF right after it
then makes an empty line of its own.
Every line is written with a final LF, including a last line that has
none.
.PP
A line is empty when nothing is left of it after this.
A line that holds only CR LF is empty, and one that holds only spaces or
tabs is not.
A CR anywhere else, such as at the end of a file with no final LF, is
kept.
Each line is held in memory whole.
.SS Numbers
Numbers are unsigned 32-bit decimal values that wrap around to 0 after
4294967295.
The
.I width
is a minimum, and a longer number is written in full.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-pba ,
and may follow
.I file
operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-w3 ,
or be the next argument.
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes
its argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
If an option is given more than once, the last one applies.
.PP
The arguments of
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.B \-w
are decimal numbers from 0 to 2147483647, with no sign, space, or
prefix.
Leading zeros are allowed, and the number is still decimal.
A value from 2147483648 to 4294967295 is reported as
.BR "not in 0..2147483647 range" ,
and any other bad argument, including a larger number, as
.BR "invalid number" .
Both errors exit with status 1.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR \fIstyle\fR, \fB\-\-body\-numbering\fR=\fIstyle\fR
Choose the lines to number by the first character of
.IR style :
.B a
for all lines,
.B t
for nonempty lines, and
.B n
for none.
Any other first character, or an empty
.IR style ,
numbers no lines, like
.BR n .
The rest of
.I style
is ignored, so the GNU form
.BI p regexp
is not supported and numbers no lines.
The default is
.BR t .
.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fIincr\fR, \fB\-\-line\-increment\fR=\fIincr\fR
Add
.I incr
to the number after each numbered line.
The default is 1.
With 0, every numbered line has the same number.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-no\-renumber
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIsep\fR, \fB\-\-number\-separator\fR=\fIsep\fR
Write
.I sep
after the number, instead of a tab.
It may be empty or several characters long.
Its length in bytes, not in display columns, sets the spaces written for
a line that is not numbered.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR \fIstart\fR, \fB\-\-starting\-line\-number\fR=\fIstart\fR
Give the first numbered line the number
.IR start ,
instead of 1.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR, \fB\-\-number\-width\fR=\fIwidth\fR
Right-align numbers in a field of at least
.I width
columns, instead of 6.
With 0, numbers have no padding.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
An unknown option, or a missing option argument, prints the usage
message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-w
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments, including option arguments, are expanded
against file names when
.B nl
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or number, a
.I file
could not be opened (a directory is reported as
.BR "Is a directory" ),
or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader closed it while
.B nl
was still writing.
If the closed pipe is found only by the final flush at exit, as with
output shorter than the output buffer, the status is 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Number the nonempty lines of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nl notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Number every line, including empty ones, in a field of 3 columns, with a
colon as the separator:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqone\en\entwo\en\(aq | nl \-ba \-w3 \-s:
  1:one
  2:
  3:two
.EE
.RE
.PP
Number from 100 in steps of 10, separated by a space:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqone\entwo\en\(aq | nl \-ba \-v 100 \-i 10 \-s \(aq \(aq
   100 one
   110 two
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find where a word occurs, with line numbers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ nl \-ba main.c | grep TODO
.EE
.RE
.PP
Number each file separately, since the count restarts at each run:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ for f in *.txt; do nl \-ba "$f"; done
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU nl:
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-v ,
and
.BR \-w ,
with their long forms, are accepted.
There are no
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-l ,
or
.BR \-n ,
so there are no logical pages, no header or footer numbering, no
joining of empty lines, and no other number format.
A delimiter line such as
.B \e:\e:\e:
is an ordinary line.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-b
recognizes only
.BR a ,
.BR t ,
and
.BR n .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-p
does nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
and
.B \-i
do not accept a negative number.
.IP \(bu 2
A CR before an LF is removed, and a NUL byte ends a line, as described
under
.BR Lines .
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, with no CR LF translation by the C
runtime.
It is
.B nl
itself that removes the CR of a CR LF line ending, so output lines end
with a bare LF.
Filter the output through
.BR unix2dos (1)
to make them CR LF.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names that busybox accepts, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
work as operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
Escape sequences in the input, such as those for color, take effect.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR unix2dos (1)
